{
  "step1_summary": "- Chief complaint: New, severe (blinding) headache ongoing for about 3 days, with marked photophobia\n- Symptoms: Onset woke up with headache 3 days ago; constant with partial relief from ibuprofen (8/10 at peak, down to ~5); all-headache pain with a emphasis around the right eye; light provokes pain; occasional brief stabbing pain on standing or rapid head movement; not waking at night; some visual disturbances described as flashing/shooting lights; no focal weakness, numbness, or aphasia; no fever, no vomiting, no cough or nasal symptoms; no recent weight loss; fatigue possibly related to pain\n- Past medical history: Prior headaches in childhood; one previous short episode lasting ~2 days that resolved with ibuprofen; no chronic headaches reported since; started combined oral contraceptive pill about 1 year ago; no known drug allergies\n- Medications/allergies: Oral contraceptive pill; ibuprofen; paracetamol as needed; no known allergies\n- Family/social history: Mother has migraines; lives alone; works from home; drinks alcohol ~weekly; non-smoker\n- Vital signs/exam findings: No vitals provided in transcript; plan includes neurological examination and fundoscopy; doctor suggested the headache could be migraine; no fever reported\n- Labs/imaging: Not mentioned\n- Additional context: Doctor discussed migraine as a possibility and noted that if headaches worsen or change, urgent evaluation (A&E) may be needed; pill use may necessitate reconsideration of therapy if migrainous features persist",
  "step2_vindicate": {
    "V": [
      "Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis (CVST) related to estrogen-containing oral contraceptive: new, persistent headache in a woman on OCP is a classic risk; morning headaches can occur; requires imaging to confirm"
    ],
    "I": [
      "Migraine with aura (or migraine without aura with photophobia): photophobia and visual phenomena (described as shooting lights), family history of migraines, episodic headaches that respond partially to NSAIDs, and normal systemic signs support migraine"
    ],
    "N": [
      "Intracranial mass with raised intracranial pressure (e.g., benign tumor or lesion): morning headaches that are persistent and bothersome, with gradual evolution, can raise ICP; visual symptoms can occur with mass effect"
    ],
    "D": [
      "Tension-type headache (considered but less likely given photophobia and focal-right-eye emphasis; could contribute to overall headache burden)"
    ],
    "I2": [
      "Hormonal/estrogen-related migraine exacerbation or medication-overuse headache: OCP use can influence migraine patterns; NSAID overuse is a potential mechanism if headaches are treated frequently"
    ],
    "C": [
      "Chiari malformation (congenital) as a very unlikely but possible cause of occipital/HA with exertional symptoms; not favored by current history but included for completeness"
    ],
    "A": [
      "Autoimmune/vasculitic processes (e.g., temporal arteritis) considered unlikely given age and lack of systemic inflammatory symptoms"
    ],
    "T": [
      "Traumatic brain injury with secondary headache (including hemorrhagic) considered unlikely given no reported head trauma"
    ],
    "E": [
      "Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (pseudotumor cerebri): more common in young to middle-aged women; presents with headaches and potential transient visual symptoms; usually obesity is a risk factor"
